To automatically prefix a string with a specific character or pattern before passing it as a regular expression in JavaScript, you can use template literals and the `RegExp` constructor. Here's an example:

```js
const prefix = '/'; // or any other desired prefix
const regexPattern = new RegExp(prefix + '(your_regex_here)');
// Now you can use regexPattern as a regular expression with your desired prefix!
```

In this example, we create a constant `prefix` with the desired prefix (in this case, `/`), and then construct a new regular expression using the `RegExp` constructor. We concatenate the `prefix` with our actual regex pattern inside the template literal to dynamically generate the final regular expression. This allows us to easily customize the regular expression prefix without having to modify multiple places in our codebase.

Here's how you could call this method:

```js
function validateUrl(url) {
  const regexPattern = new RegExp(/^https?:\/\/[^\s$<>]+$/);
  return regexPattern.test(url);
}

// Using the auto-prefixed version
const prefix = '/';
const regexPattern = new RegExp(prefix + '(^https?:\\/\\/[^\\s$<>]+$)');
function validateUrlWithPrefix(url) {
  return regexPattern.test(url);
}

console.log(validateUrl("https://example.com")); // true
console.log(validateUrlWithPrefix("/https://example.com")); // true
```

As you can see, we can now call the `validateUrlWithPrefix()` function with the auto-prefixed regular expression and pass in the URL with the prefix included. The rest of the function remains the same. This way, we don't have to modify the original function to handle the prefix, which makes it more flexible and reusable across different parts of our application.

